            <ArticleTitle>Characterization of Prognostic Factors and the Efficacy of Adjuvant S-1 Chemotherapy in Patients with Post-surgery Extrahepatic Bile Duct Cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>7049-7056</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND/AIM">There is no clear consensus on the type of adjuvant therapy that should be used for patients with extrahepatic bile duct cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND METHODS">Two hundred and seventy-one patients that had undergone surgical resection for extrahepatic bile duct cancer composed the study cohort. Demographics, treatments, and relationships between the potential prognostic factors and survival rates were analyzed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS">The overall 3-year and 5-year survival rates for post-surgery extrahepatic bile duct cancer patients were 49.0% and 35.4%, respectively. Multivariate analysis revealed that regional lymph node metastasis was an independent negative prognostic factor. We observed a significant correlation between node-positive extrahepatic bile duct cancer and postoperative local recurrence, liver metastasis, peritoneal dissemination, and post-surgery lymph node metastasis. Adjuvant S-1 chemotherapy showed a favorable hazard ratio in patients with lymph node metastases or positive vascular invasion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION">We recommend the use of adjuvant S-1 therapy in patients with lymph node metastases or microvascular invasion.</AbstractText>
